.TH velnet "" "Command"
.SH NAME
velnet \- what a vellum drawing is connected as
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBvelnet\fR \fIfile.d ...\fR
.br
\fBvelnet \-bom\fR \fIfile.d ...\fR
.br
\fBvelnet \-spice\fR \fIfile.d ...\fR
.br
\fBvelnet \-renum\fR [\fB\-base\fR \fIn\fR] \fIfile.d\fR > \fIout.d\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
velnet -- what a vellum drawing is connected as
Usage:	velnet file.d ...		the netlist
	velnet -bom file.d ...		the parts list
	velnet -spice file.d ...	a SPICE 2G6 deck
	velnet -renum [-base n] f.d	canonical designators
	Named nets MERGE across the sheet set; the parts list
	is ONE list for the whole design.  - is standard input.
.HE
.B velnet
extracts the CONNECTIVITY of drawings made with
.BR vellum :
wires unioned by endpoint, junction dots joined, symbol pins
attached, nets named.
The four forms below are four ways of writing down the same walk, so
a netlist and a parts list can never disagree with each other.
.PP
Every form takes the sheet SET: named nets merge across sheets (a net
called VBUS on sheet 1 is the same conductor as VBUS on sheet 3), and
a design gets ONE parts list.
The file name
.B \-
is standard input.
.PP
.B The netlist (no option)
.br
One line per net, with R1.2-style pins; a library that NAMES its pins
prints Q1.B instead.
.B GND
and
.B VCC
stencils and
.B N
net-name markers name nets.
Designator-less stencils (rails, taps, off-page markers) carry nets
but are never listed as pins.
Single-pin nets and unconnected pins are warned about on standard
error; to make them FAIL a build, use
.BR velcheck .
.PP
.B \-bom
.br
The bill of materials over the whole set: count, stencil, value and
the designators, sorted.
A designated part with no value prints its value as ``-'' \- and is a
finding in
.BR velcheck ,
which is where that belongs.
.PP
.B \-spice
.br
A SPICE 2G6 deck on standard output: the same nets as the netlist,
numbered (0 for the GND-named net), one card per designated R/C/L/D/Q.
Values are parsed from the value field \- ``4k7'', ``100n'',
``2.2M'' \- where the suffix letter doubles as the decimal point, and
the schematic's M emits as MEG.
A D or Q card ends with its stencil CODE as the model name unless its
value names one.
Pin ORDER is the library's, except that a library which NAMES its pins
B/C/E (or A/K) orders the card the way SPICE wants; the stock
.B discrete
library does.
.PP
Everything the subset cannot say is SKIPPED AND COUNTED on standard
error (``dropped: 1 OPA, 2 J''): a silent partial netlist is how
simulations lie.
Models are yours by design \- the deck ends
.B .END
and a wrapper file
.B .INCLUDE s
it beside the
.B .MODEL
lines.
.PP
.B \-renum
.br
Canonical designators: symbols sorted by (y, x), numbered per prefix
from
.BI \-base " n"
(default 1), and the whole drawing re-emitted on standard output.
Designators live nowhere else in the format \- nets are positional \-
so the rewrite cannot dangle a reference.
ONE sheet per run; a set renumbers sheet by sheet with
.B \-base
carrying the count across, because the Makefile owns the set.
.SH EXAMPLES
.DS
.ta 8n
	velnet amp1.d amp2.d
	velnet -bom amp?.d > parts.txt
	velnet -spice amp?.d > deck.cir
	velnet -renum -base 1 amp1.d > new.d && mv new.d amp1.d
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B vellum ,
.B velcheck ,
.B velinfo ,
.B symedit
.SH NOTES
The conductor questions are only meaningful on a sheet that draws
CIRCUITS.
A diagram sheet \- flowchart, floor plan, structure chart \- uses
stencils whose pins are attachment points, not terminals; this
program will still walk it, but
.B velcheck
deliberately does not judge it.
